{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792699780,"nonce":"TZ2W7JG3JbXNv+7LoPoxNT78z7A=","title":"Cranelift: native symbol signature differs between a call and its declaration for bool and narrow integers","message":"A program whose tiered (OSR / first-call) compile calls a native symbol with a bool argument, after a hot loop, panicked with IncompatibleSignature: the external declaration gives bool/u8/u16 parameters zero-extension (sext for i8/i16) but the symbol-call path built the same symbol's signature without any extension, and the JIT module rejects two signatures for one symbol. Reproducer: a TypeScript loop of 3,000,000 iterations followed by console.log(s, s \u003e 1).\n\nFix: the symbol-call path now applies the same extension rule, from the ZRTL type tag when a signature is registered and from the HIR argument type otherwise. Regression test: boolean_arguments_survive_tier_up in crates/zyntax_ts/tests/native_bool.rs. Other backends (LLVM) not examined for the same mismatch.","files":null}]}